WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.430
He’s tall, thin, faceless, and wears a dark
suit.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:05.509
Once he’s in your life—you’re doomed.

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:10.890
If you try to learn more about him or even
think about him, it only makes it easier for

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.179
him to find you.

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:16.350
He’s the notorious Internet monster—Slender
Man.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:22.010
This 21st-century monster gained popularity
as more and more content was created and shared

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:28.350
about him, enough so that he jumped the digital
border and began to infiltrate other media.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.210
Video games, comic books, and movies show
him stalking humans.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.820
People also dress up like him and hunt for
him in the woods.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.780
Then...there’s the tragic attempted murder
of a 12-year-old girl in 2014 that made him

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.579
a household name.

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:48.629
Unlike so many other monsters we know exactly
when this one was created and by whom, a rarity

00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:50.609
made possible by the Internet.

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:55.320
His obscurity is what makes him scary, but
the real impact of this monster is how he

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.739
changed folklore forever.

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:03.140
[Monstrum intro.]

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:09.270
On June 8, 2009 the online forum Something
Awful posted a photoshop challenge: “turn

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:13.820
ordinary photographs into creepy-looking images
through digital manipulation.”

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.530
User Victor Surge, whose real name is Eric
Knudsen, responded on June 10

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.340
with two black and white photographs.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.020
The first photograph shows a group of teenagers
walking towards the camera.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:30.611
Behind them, you can barely make out a tall,
blurry figure lurking in the background.

00:01:30.611 --> 00:01:33.510
The teens seem unaware of his presence.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.240
The second photograph depicts a more clear
image of Slender Man.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:42.680
It shows children happily playing in a playground,
but in the background under a tree, a shadowy

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:43.680
fig-yur looms.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.180
In this photo, it appears that Slender Man
also has tentacles, almost overnight, Slender

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.270
Man became an internet sensation.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.560
As his mythos evolved, some common traits
emerged.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.810
Slender Man is described as noticeably taller
than the average human with an oddly proportioned

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:00.810
body.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:05.420
He’s very thin with long arms and legs,
and in some accounts, he can sprout tentacles

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.930
from his back.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:13.469
He wears a suit and has no facial features
or hair and his skin is unnaturally pale.

00:02:13.469 --> 00:02:16.480
Anyone who sees Slender Man is then stalked
by him.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.310
So it becomes a sort of creepy loop; the more
he is seen, the more commonly he appears.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.920
So the fact that I’m making this video means
I’m already doomed.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.910
What made Victor Surge’s post stand out
more than other submissions was that it was

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.340
the first to create a narrative that accompanied
the images.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.769
He posted the photos with the following text:
“One of two recovered photographs from the

00:02:36.769 --> 00:02:42.120
Stirling City Library blaze Notable for being
taken the day which fourteen children vanished

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.249
and for what is referred to as ‘The Slender
Man.’

00:02:45.249 --> 00:02:48.060
Deformities cited as film defects by officials.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.140
Fire at library occurred one week later.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:53.150
Actual photograph confiscated as evidence.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:58.370
1986, photographer: Mary Thomas, missing since
June 13th, 1986.”

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.689
Slenderman was born from those words.

00:03:01.689 --> 00:03:04.629
The context crafted the myth.

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:08.799
Something Awful users instantly latched on
to the mysterious monster’s story.

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:13.340
The Slender Man post sparked enthusiastic
comments, with many noting how it would make

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.969
for a great tv show or a book.

00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:19.930
Others quickly jumped on to the Slender Man
bandwagon by posting their own accounts of

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:20.930
the creature.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:26.890
Within days, the thread became almost exclusively
made up of “real” Slender Man legends.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:31.790
One notable post from a user with the screen
name ce gars, mentions his own investigation

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.340
into a film project that caused his friend
to become increasingly disturbed.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:41.629
A few days later, the film project in question
was uploaded to YouTube, under the title Marble

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:42.629
Hornets.

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:47.909
It features a story, inspired by Slender Man,
about a boogeyman figure capable of distorting

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:52.569
audio and video recordings causing the film’s
creator to be driven insane.

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:57.569
It turned out that ce gars was Troy Wagner,
who crafted the series with his friends.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:02.549
Marble Hornets became a huge part of the monster’s
mythos and was incredibly successful, totaling

00:04:02.549 --> 00:04:05.169
more than 90 found-footage episodes.

00:04:05.169 --> 00:04:10.189
It even helped inspire two Slender Man video
games.

00:04:10.189 --> 00:04:14.609
Slender Man made his Creepypasta debut on
January 14, 2010.

00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:18.989
Creepypasta developed as a place to collect
spooky stories.

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:24.560
Users would“copy” and “paste” “creepy”
texts from other web sites such as 4chan.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.780
Some people argue that Creepypasta and others
such as the /nosleep subreddit obscure the

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:30.780
truth.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:36.460
They are veiled behind a faux authenticity—fictional
stories, often accompanied by seemingly authentic

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:38.400
images—that are treated as real.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.830
But as a scholar of the Gothic, I can tell
you that treating mythical stories as actual

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:45.199
accounts is nothing new at all.

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:50.120
Some original Gothic novels were published
and falsely advertised as “translations”

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:55.280
of “newly discovered” manuscripts by real
authors, or alongside claims that they were

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.590
true accounts from some anonymous writer.

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:04.830
But, in reality they were completely fabricated.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.910
More recently, found footage horror films
[Blair Witch, etc.] are another example of

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:13.250
how the lines of fiction and truth are intentionally
blurred.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.319
Victor Surge admits Slender Man was made up.

00:05:15.319 --> 00:05:20.129
An idea he crafted from all the things that
scare him with a name he says he “thought

00:05:20.129 --> 00:05:21.120
up on the fly”

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.740
He does admit he used the Tall Man from the
Phantasm films as the source material for

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:28.060
the first Slender Man images.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:33.539
But by keeping the details vague, he allowed
others to fill in the gaps of his creation

00:05:33.539 --> 00:05:35.580
with their own fears.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:38.530
Slender Man shares similarities to a lot of
other pre-existing characters.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:43.620
He’s been compared to the Pied Piper, Buffy
the Vampire Slayer’s the Gentlemen, The

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.520
Silence from Dr. Who, the Men in Black, and
Jack Skellington.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:51.550
Tales about him contain some elements of the
Blair Witch Project and H.P.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:52.940
Lovecraft stories.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:58.560
There’s even a pre-existing Japanese yokai
that appears human but has no facial features,

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.470
just like Slender Man.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:04.740
You may have heard that Slender Man sightings
have been happening for hundreds of years,

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.960
that he appears in old texts and art, from
Germanic mythology to Canadian folklore, Norse

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.680
mythology, and Egyptian hieroglyphics.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.300
It’s enticing to think that we can discover
a monster that’s been secretly haunting

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:17.300
us for years.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.250
That’s a cool story.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:20.590
But none of that is true.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:23.510
He didn’t exist before 2009.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:25.930
Take this image for example.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:30.849
Some people claim this is an image of Slender
Man dating back to the 16th century, but in

00:06:30.849 --> 00:06:35.950
reality it’s a photoshopped image based
on existing prints from that time period.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.909
The original comes from a series of danse macabre images

00:06:38.909 --> 00:06:41.870
meant to show that death is universal.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:43.120
This one?

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.009
Another photoshop of an image taken from the
same artist.

00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:50.990
Similar to other monsters, such as Windigo
and Tokoloshe, Slender Man has been used as

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.090
a scapegoat for deviant behavior.

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:58.060
In 2014, two girls attempted to murder their
friend because they allegedly thought it would

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.770
attract the attention of Slender Man.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:05.449
Such a dramatic and violent crime drew the
attention of the media, and the once notorious

00:07:05.449 --> 00:07:11.889
Internet monster became a familiar story offline
as well, causing moral panic and fear.

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:16.490
Slender Man’s faceless features allow us
to transcribe pretty much any of our fears

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:17.840
onto him.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:19.199
He’s uncanny.

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:22.639
His human silhouette and common clothing makes
him familiar.

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:24.710
But something is off.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:25.990
His dark suit is unsettling.

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:29.090
There could be a couple of reasons why:

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:32.969
It shows him polished and put together, and
intentional in what he does.

00:07:32.969 --> 00:07:36.960
He is self aware, patiently waiting and watching
his victims.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:43.460
There’s also the association of black suits
with death, something we see commonly at funerals.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.639
And then there’s the idea that the suit
symbolizes power and authority.

00:07:46.639 --> 00:07:49.990
It’s anonymous and timeless.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:54.620
The idea that Slender Man specifically targets
children feeds into our fear that there are

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:58.710
evil strangers ready to kidnap our kids.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.629
Slender Man’s the new version of “don’t
take candy from a stranger,” a lesson that

00:08:02.629 --> 00:08:08.310
keeps kids safe by instilling a healthy wariness
for adults they don’t know.

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.750
Slender Man is always watching you, even if
you don’t know it.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:16.180
This parallels our more legitimate fears of
being constantly watched by cameras and other

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.950
technologies that monitor our day to day actions.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:24.270
Heck, even the Internet where Slender Man
was first created constantly logs so much

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.749
of what we do.

00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:31.330
Slender Man takes the fear of something that
seems abstract, like a story on the Internet,

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:35.419
to something more concrete like a man outside
your window at night.

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:38.800
Slender Man could have been nothing more than
a scary, made up story.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.611
But when people misread it, treat it as real,
the lines between what is authentic and what

00:08:43.611 --> 00:08:47.210
is false blur to dangerous levels.

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:50.910
Technology allows anyone to make fake things
appear real.

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:54.940
Photoshopped images, computer edited video
footage, and falsified documents can spread

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.150
quickly and are seen as legitimate.

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.800
And anyone can contribute.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:04.389
Slender Man represents an important shift
in folklore where myths are crafted at a blistering

00:09:04.389 --> 00:09:07.230
pace across social, cultural, and physical
boundaries.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:13.570
It’s a kind of digital Gothic where the
haunted house becomes the haunted everything.

00:09:13.570 --> 00:09:17.430
Slender Man and other digital folklore succeeds
because of the Internet.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:19.951
These myths spread like wildfire through social
media.

00:09:19.951 --> 00:09:26.580
The time stamps of online folklore allow us
to examine real-time discussions, responses,

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.362
and reactions to mythology as it’s created.

00:09:28.362 --> 00:09:34.310
It gives us an exact account of how the story
developed in real time—something that doesn’t

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:36.400
didn’t exist in traditional folklore studies
before.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:41.430
I think this is part of what makes Slender
Man so popular, what gives him life—he can

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:45.190
be made by anyone for anyone.

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:49.420
Slender Man unintentionally revolutionized
how horror stories are told.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:54.000
He opened up the doors for anyone with an
internet connection to help create a legend.

